WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.560
Most of us are used to the idea of an actor
getting a role and, you know, acting the part.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:09.690
We'll even accept a stunt double for scenes
where the actor needs to jump out of a plane.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:13.740
But for the most part, if we're watching Gary
Busey we want to see Gary Busey.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.840
Wait, why do we want to see Gary Busey again?

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:22.040
"hahaha."

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:26.920
As it turns out, we're not always watching
who we think we're watching, and that happens

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.300
much more often than we realize.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:32.580
There have been plenty of times when actors
were swapped out, and us in the audience never

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:33.260
even noticed.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:34.880
Natalie Portman

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.920
The heated kiss between Thor and Jane Foster
in Thor: The Dark World's post-credits scene

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:45.190
is one of the steamiest moments in all the
Marvel movies, and with good reason: actor

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:48.620
Chris Hemsworth isn't making out with his
costar, Natalie Portman.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:53.320
The scene was shot after principal photography
had wrapped, and Natalie Portman was unavailable.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.760
Portman told the New York Daily News, "I couldn't
get there because I was working on my own

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.720
film...and so they put his wife in my wig
and costume, that's why it was so passionate."

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:08.240
Yup, Hemsworth was locking lips with his real-life
wife, Spanish actress and Fast &amp; Furious star

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:08.960
Elsa Pataky.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.330
The switcheroo certainly saved a lot of trouble
for the crew.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:16.470
While filming other kissing scenes in the
Thor series, Portman said that, since she's

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:20.750
so short, "They usually had a ramp for me
to walk up so I could land somewhere near

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:21.360
his face."

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.160
Because that's the only way to make this turn
into something like this.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:27.740
Bruce Lee

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:34.200
After Bruce Lee's untimely death on July 20,
1973, one of his greatest passion projects

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:38.920
was left unfinished: Game of Death, of which
Lee had already filmed about 40 minutes.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:43.880
Five years later, director Robert Clouse picked
up the pieces and was forced to create a brand-new

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:49.850
plot using Lee's previously filmed scenes,
shots from older Bruce Lee projects, and even

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:51.900
footage from Lee's real-life funeral.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:57.720
In addition to archival film, Clouse also
hired stand-ins to play Billy Lo, Lee's character

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.600
in the new version of Game of Death.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:05.380
In Clouse's Game of Death, Lo both receives
plastic surgery after a brutal fight and later

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:10.179
fakes his own death and wears a disguise,
making it easy for Clouse to replace Lee with

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:11.209
other actors.

00:02:11.209 --> 00:02:13.500
Although some parts weren't exactly seamless…

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:19.320
"Well, sitting right there by the telephone,
just waiting for you to call.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:21.800
Patience, that's not one of our virtues."

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:23.920
Michael Pitt

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:29.580
It's hard to imagine anyone more villainous
than Hannibal Lecter, but the TV series Hannibal

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.160
gave us just such a character in Mason Verger.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:37.580
As the sadistic meat-packing tycoon, Boardwalk
Empire star Michael Pitt spends the latter

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.480
half of the show's second season tormenting
the other characters.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.900
Pitt delivers a terrifying performance as
Verger, who showrunner Bryan Fuller called

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.640
"The Joker to Hannibal's Batman."

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:52.140
But while Mason Verger returned in Season
3, Michael Pitt didn't.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:56.920
Joe Anderson took over the role and had to
spend the entirety of the season covered in

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:57.880
heavy prosthetics.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:03.440
Fortunately, Anderson's vocal performance
was flawless, resulting in a seamless transition

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:04.400
between the two actors.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:09.940
"There, now we can talk face to face."

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:12.560
Nicholas Brendon

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:18.709
Nicholas Brendon appeared in all but one episode
of Buffy the Vampire Slayer, and yet, surprisingly,

00:03:18.709 --> 00:03:24.240
he wasn't the only person to play Xander Harris,
Buffy's geeky pal and perennial punching bag.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:29.620
Brendon's twin brother, who uses the stage
name Kelly Donovan, often took Brendon's place

00:03:29.629 --> 00:03:34.379
when the actor wasn't available or when Buffy's
supernatural shenanigans required more than

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:35.900
one Xander on screen at once.

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:40.920
Donovan made his most memorable appearance
on Buffy in the fifth season episode "The

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:45.860
Replacement," in which a demon creates a second
Xander who threatens to take over the original's

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:46.360
life.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:51.440
In the episode "Intervention," Donovan replaced
Brendon entirely during some action scenes

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:53.820
when Brendon had pneumonia and couldn't come
to the set.

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:55.420
Gene Hackman

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:01.600
It might be hard to imagine now, but in the
'70s, only one big-budget superhero franchise

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:02.740
ruled the silver screen.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.820
Even before the first Superman film became
a breakout hit, director Richard Donner was

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:09.280
already working on a sequel.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:15.059
Unfortunately, during filming, Donner clashed
with producers Ilya and Alexander Salkind

00:04:15.059 --> 00:04:20.250
about the film's budget, and although Donner
had already filmed roughly 75% of Superman

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:25.160
II during production of the first film, he
was replaced by A Hard Day's Night's Richard

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:25.660
Lester.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:31.940
In order to secure a director's credit, Lester
reshot quite a bit of Donner's footage, substantially

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:35.000
changing Superman II's story and tone in the
process.

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:39.180
The way the Salkinds treated Donner didn't
sit well with the cast.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:43.530
Reportedly, Christopher Reeve was openly furious
about Donner's dismissal.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:48.060
According to the book Superman vs. Hollywood,
Gene Hackman, who played Lex Luthor in both

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:52.600
films, flat-out refused to show up to finish
shooting Superman II.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:57.360
With Hackman absent, Lester and the Salkinds
were forced to use body doubles and voice

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.480
impersonators just to finish the movie.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:00.780
Josh Pence

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:07.180
In 2010, Armie Hammer made headlines—and
picked up a couple of awards—for his portrayal

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:12.160
of identical twins Cameron and Tyler Winklevoss
in The Social Network, but he couldn't have

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:12.660
done it alone.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.500
On the set, Tyler was actually played by Gangster
Squad star Josh Pence.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:22.560
Later, Pence's face was digitally replaced
with Hammer's by the special effects team.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:27.420
In order to prepare for the dual roles, Hammer
and Pence attended a "twin boot camp," where

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:32.920
together they worked out each twin's particular
quirks and mannerisms, and while Pence's career

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:37.960
hasn't taken off like Hammer's, he's become
more positive about his Social Network experience

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:38.460
over time.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:40.220
Josh Dallas

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:46.180
For such a small role, Marvel Studios sure
had a lot of trouble casting Fandral the Dashing,

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:47.620
the Asgardian warrior.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:52.640
First, Chuck star Zachary Levi was set to
play the goateed Asgardian, but had to drop

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:56.560
out when NBC ordered more episodes of Chuck's
third season.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:00.840
Next, Marvel cast Queen of the Damned star
Stuart Townsend in the role, but Townsend

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:04.960
departed shortly before filming due to mysterious
"creative differences."

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:10.550
Marvel eventually found its Fandral in a then-unknown
actor named Josh Dallas, who finally brought

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:12.560
the classic character to the screen.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:14.020
But the trouble didn't stop there.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:18.920
Between Thor's premiere and the start of filming
on Thor: The Dark World, Dallas landed the

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:22.840
role of Prince Charming on ABC's fairytale
drama Once Upon a Time.

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:27.020
Because of Once Upon a Time's rigorous shooting
schedule, Dallas couldn't join his fellow

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:30.880
Warriors for their big screen return, and
Marvel needed to recast the part.

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:31.760
Their choice?

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:35.240
Zachary Levi, of course, who was finally free
to take the role.

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:38.280
"Perhaps next time we should start with the
big one."

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:40.500
Penelope Cruz

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:46.020
Mónica Cruz has been an accomplished dancer
and actress for years, and while she's had

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:50.470
featured roles in several foreign movies,
she's probably most famous in America for

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:55.180
her part in Pirates of the Caribbean: On Stranger
Tides—in which she played her sister, Penélope.

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:59.990
Penélope took over from Keira Knightley in
Rob Marshall's 2010 sequel and played the

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:02.120
part of Angelica, Jack Sparrow's love interest.

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:05.020
While filming, though, Penélope learned that
she was pregnant.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:09.960
In order to minimize the risk to Penélope's
unborn baby, Marshall hired Mónica, who looks

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:12.680
a lot like her older sister, to serve as a
stand-in.

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:14.430
In an interview with The Telegraph, Penélope
said:

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:18.520
"I did a couple of months of training and
did what I was able to do, but I couldn't

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:20.520
do everything, just what was safe.

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:22.520
Monica came at the end to do some scenes.

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:26.800
She's a dancer and very good with a sword
because she's done a film herself."

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:31.590
Neither Penélope nor Mónica returned in
Pirates of the Caribbean: Dead Men Tell No

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:36.660
Tales, but the Cruz family is still a big
part of the franchise: Javier Bardem, Penélope's

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:38.460
husband, plays the film's lead villain.

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:40.320
Lena Headey

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:45.380
As Cersei Lannister, Lena Headey plays the
ultimate villain on HBO's Game of Thrones.

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:49.920
So viewers watched with a bit of sadistic
glee when the character was literally and

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:55.370
figuratively dressed down… and then paraded
through the streets of King's Landing in nothing

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:56.580
but smears of filth.

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:01.830
The scene is intense and humiliating, and
drawn out so the audience can experience her

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:02.960
awkward humiliation.

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:07.520
The craziest part, however, is that we're
not even watching Headey in the scene.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:12.710
The entire walk is performed by a body double
seamlessly merged with Headey's facial expressions

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:16.460
and body language to make one awkward, horrible
walk to the Red Keep.

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:18.180
Brandon Lee

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:23.140
The son of legendary martial artist Bruce
Lee, the charming and talented Brandon Lee

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:25.000
was set to take Hollywood by storm.

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:29.900
Sadly, while filming his breakout role in
The Crow, Lee was shot by a prop gun that

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:32.140
had a dummy round left in the chamber.

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:33.300
He died later at the hospital.

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:39.180
To complete the film, Lee's friend and stunt
double Chad Stahelski stood in for him while

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:41.460
special effects were used to give him Lee's
face.

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:45.700
Meanwhile, another stunt double helped finish
out some of the action scenes.

00:08:45.710 --> 00:08:50.090
The result is a movie in which Lee's role
is perfect, and there's no indication of the

00:08:50.090 --> 00:08:51.260
tragedy that befell him.

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:53.240
Jennifer Aniston

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:59.160
Friends was a massive success and Jennifer
Aniston is arguably the biggest star it produced.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:02.990
Which makes it all the more unusual that it
took years after the show was off the air

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:07.610
for someone to finally notice an episode where
Aniston inexplicably disappears.

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:12.260
A fan watching a Friends marathon happened
to notice a strange scene in “The One with

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:13.620
the Mugging,” from the ninth season.

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:18.100
Aniston is standing next to Matt Leblanc,
except it's not Aniston at all.

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:23.900
Seems that Aniston's stand-in was on set that
day and, for whatever reason, her scene wasn't

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:25.570
edited to remove her from the shot.

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:28.520
The presence of a stand-in isn't such a crazy
thing.

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:32.110
After all, stand-ins are on set to replace
actors when they're not available.

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:34.720
It's just that they usually don't end up in
the final cut.

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:36.580
Shemp Howard

00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:41.530
The Three Stooges were under contract to produce
eight films in 1956.

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:45.920
Unfortunately, four films into their contract,
Shemp Howard died of a heart attack at age

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:46.420
60.

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:49.940
And in the 1950s, no one gave a damn if you
died or not.

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:53.840
Your contract needed to be fulfilled, and
thus was born the "Fake Shemp."

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:59.380
Producer Jules White set about producing four
brand new films, each starring Shemp, by cutting

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:04.650
together old footage with new scenes featuring
Joe Palma, an actor who had long filled supporting

00:10:04.650 --> 00:10:05.860
roles in the Stooges' films.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:08.680
They just filmed him from behind or with his
face obscured.

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:11.320
"Hey Shemp, we're in here!

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:14.060
We got a lot of work to do, so shake a leg!

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:15.020
"Hold your horses, will ya?"

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:19.720
The deception was so obvious that its legacy
still lives on today...

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:21.520
The Cast of Evil Dead

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:26.990
The term "Fake Shemp" was actually coined
by director Sam Raimi because he needed to

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:29.960
use quite a few of them during production
of Evil Dead.

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:35.180
As star Bruce Campbell recounted in his memoir
If Chins Could Kill, because the film was

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:39.870
so ultra low-budget, Raimi and the actors
couldn't always work on the same schedule.

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:43.710
Raimi needed to film what he could, when he
could, and with whom he could.

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:47.440
This meant no fewer than 18 stand-ins were
used during production.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:52.880
That's pretty incredible, as the end result
shows no sign that so many extra people were

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:53.380
involved.

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:56.040
Even though the film is still notoriously
ridiculous.

00:10:57.380 --> 00:10:58.220
"Shut up!"

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:04.820
"hahaha."

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:06.500
Harrison Ford

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:11.600
On the set of Indiana Jones and the Temple
of Doom, Harrison Ford suffered a pretty severe

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:14.790
back injury that sidelined him for several
weeks of filming.

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:18.230
Steven Spielberg, however, was prepared for
any eventuality.

00:11:18.230 --> 00:11:21.600
Ford was swapped with his longtime stunt double,
Vic Armstrong.

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:26.900
Armstrong acted so much like Ford that he
managed to confuse Spielberg on-set more than

00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:27.400
once.

00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:32.500
A number of action scenes were filmed without
Ford, including the epic fight between Indy

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:35.060
and the massive warrior on the mine conveyor
belt.

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:36.400
Thanks for watching!

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:39.270
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:41.390
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

